    "text": "A review of the WorldLines paper's benchmark tasks (Baseline, Overwrite, Prune, Partial Observability) shows that each task definition includes either partial observability, state overwrite, or both; no task variant is described that removes these elements to isolate long-term memory translation as a standalone challenge.",
    "topic": "Benchmark task composition"
  },
  {
    "text": "The paper introduces memory-to-plan translation as a method to address the challenges of partial observability and state corruption (Section 1 and 3), thereby framing it as an integrated solution component rather than a separable problem domain.",
    "topic": "Memory-to-plan translation framing"
  },
  {
    "text": "Because statement A asserts the absence of stripped-down task variants and statement B asserts inseparability by design, the two statements are consistent; the contradiction stems from an ambiguity in interpreting whether 'inseparable as a standalone challenge' is a claim about the paper's scope or an inherent property.",
    "topic": "Resolution of perceived contradiction"
  }
]
